 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the labor law, in relation to an annual pay data report",
    "basePrintNo": "S4047",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4047-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 31,
       "fullName": "Robert Jackson",
       "imgName": "1050_Robert_Jackson.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1230,
       "sessionMemberId": 2017,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "JACKSON"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §194-c, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4047\n\nSPONSOR: FERNANDEZ\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the labor law, in relation to an annual pay data report\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo establish an annual pay data report for private employers\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends Labor Law by adding section 194-a to require private\nemployers of one hundred or more employees and who are required to file\nan annual Employer Information Report to submit a pay data report\n\nSection 2: Sets forth the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPay discrimination does not just affect one particular group of people.\nIts effect is felt by all families in New York State and the New York\neconomy at large. For example, the number of children with working moth-\ners living in poverty would be nearly cut in half, dropping from 5.6\nmillion to 3.1 million. Pay equity would represent approximately 16\ntimes what federal and state government's spent on Temporary Assistance\nto Needy Families in 2016.\n\nAlthough there are legitimate and lawful reasons for paying some employ-\nees more than others, pay discrimination continues to exist.  While New\nYork has the smallest pay gap of any state in the country at 89 cents,\nfor every dollar a man makes, there is still a long way to go. Women who\nmake the most amount of money, only earn 74% of a male counterparts in a\nsimilar job.\n\nThe Obama Administration announced a proposed revision to the Employer\nInformation Report (EEO-1) to include the reporting of pay data by\ngender, race and ethnicity beginning in 2018. However, in August 2017,\nthe Trump Administration put a halt to the implementation of this new\nrule. This would allow employers in New York State to keep pay inequi-\nties \"hidden from sight\".\n\nBy enacting this bill, pay data will be compiled and aggregated in New\nYork. Pay data reporting purposes to allow designated state agencies to\ncollect wage data to more efficiently identify wage patterns and allow\nfor targeted enforcement of equal pay or discrimination laws, when\nappropriate.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-2022: S453 / A1988\n\n2020: S4065 / A6790\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law. Effective immediately, the addition, amendment\nand/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary for the implementation\nof this act on its effective date are authorized to be made and\ncompleted on or before such effective date.\n",
